abstract: >-
  Classical computation tracks two things about a run: how long it takes and how
  much space it uses. It never tracks whether the result was earned. The Thiele
  Machine adds that third axis, where structure is first-class, paid-for state,
  so a computation cannot treat a fact as established unless the trace paid for
  the right to it. It computes exactly the same functions as a Turing machine.
  What is new is the axis and one law on it. The sharpest instance is
  certification: the step that turns a result from unverified to verified is
  priced, by a single rule called A2, inside the step relation itself, not in a
  checker a later program could skip or fake. No classical step rule has a field
  to read that cost or that certificate, so on a Turing, register, or lambda
  machine A2 can only be a checker run beside the program, never the law of its
  moves. That is what makes classical computation the structurally lossy
  projection of a substrate that carries the law. The repository contains the
  machine-checked Coq kernel (zero project-local axioms, zero admits, 3,937
  theorems probed across 277 files), an OCaml runner extracted from it, a
  Kami/Bluespec/Verilog RTL realization, and the accompanying monograph. The
  software is licensed Apache-2.0; the monograph and distillation are licensed
  CC-BY-SA-4.0.
